(l) distribute the Original Base under a license that removes or
contradicts the restrictions contained in this License; or

(m) intentionally structure a transaction or distribution method for the
primary purpose of circumventing the restrictions on selling the
Original Base.

6. WHAT COUNTS AS A DERIVATIVE PROJECT

6.1 A Derivative Project must contain meaningful original development
created by You or Your contributors.

6.2 Examples of meaningful development may include:

(a) substantial new functionality;

(b) new systems or features;

(c) significant changes to the architecture;

(d) substantial new source code;

(e) substantial user-interface development;

(f) new configuration or management systems;

(g) new integrations;

(h) new tools or systems;

(i) substantial performance or functionality improvements; or

(j) other original development that materially changes or expands the
purpose or functionality of the Base.

6.3 The following alone will generally NOT constitute sufficient
development to create a Derivative Project:

(a) changing the name;

(b) changing the logo;

(c) changing colors;

(d) changing a few strings;

(e) changing advertisements or branding;

(f) changing configuration values;

(g) removing the original attribution;

(h) making minor cosmetic changes;

(i) making insignificant source-code changes; or

(j) performing other changes whose primary purpose is to make the
Original Base appear different while retaining substantially the
same functionality.

6.4 No specific percentage of changed code shall determine whether a
project is a Derivative Project.

The determination shall instead consider the overall nature of the
project, including the amount and significance of original development,
the functionality added, and whether the commercial value primarily comes
from the Original Base or from the new work created by You.

